---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Buildprint docs"
  description="Developer-first guide to Buildprint packet files, markdown structure, read order, and getting started."
>
  <main class="docs-page docs-site" aria-labelledby="docs-title">
    <div class="container docs-site-shell">
      <aside class="docs-sidebar" aria-label="Docs navigation">
        <div class="docs-sidebar-inner">
          <a class="docs-sidebar-title" href="/docs/">Buildprint docs</a>
          <nav>
            <a href="#overview">Overview</a>
            <a href="#anatomy">Packet anatomy</a>
            <a href="#product">Product Buildprint</a>
            <a href="#capability">Capability Buildprint</a>
            <a href="#read-order">Read order</a>
            <a href="#install-agb">Install AGB</a>
            <a href="#getting-started">Getting started</a>
            <a href="#create-buildprint">Create a Buildprint</a>
          </nav>
        </div>
      </aside>

      <article class="docs-content">
        <header class="docs-doc-header">
          <p class="docs-breadcrumb">Docs / Basics</p>
          <h1 id="docs-title">Buildprint basics</h1>
          <p>A Buildprint is a folder of markdown and structured files that tells a coding agent what to build, what to preserve, what order to follow, and how to prove the work is done.</p>
        </header>

        <section id="overview" class="docs-doc-section">
          <h2>Overview</h2>
          <p>Use a Buildprint when a normal prompt is too vague. Instead of asking an agent to infer the job from scratch, give it a packet with a canonical start file, ordered phases, constraints, and verification rules.</p>
          <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Rule of thumb:</strong>
            <span>If the work should be repeatable, reviewable, or handed to another agent later, it belongs in a Buildprint.</span>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="anatomy" class="docs-doc-section">
          <h2>Packet anatomy</h2>
          <p>The exact files vary by packet type, but the basic structure is simple.</p>
          <pre><code>example-buildprint/
|-- BUILDPRINT.md       # canonical start file
|-- README.md           # human overview
|-- blueprint.yaml      # machine-readable route
|-- 00-questions.md     # alignment before coding
|-- 01-project-setup.md # setup, commands, constraints
|-- 02-ui-identity.md   # product/UI rules when needed
|-- 03-phases/
|   |-- phase-index.yaml
|   |-- phase-flow.md
|   |-- 01-contracts.md
|   |-- 02-implementation.md
|   `-- 03-verification.md
`-- HANDOVER.md         # final receipt</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="product" class="docs-doc-section">
          <h2>Product Buildprint</h2>
          <p>Use this when the agent is building a whole app, product, tool, or large product surface.</p>
          <pre><code>product-buildprint/
|-- BUILDPRINT.md
|-- 00-questions.md
|-- 01-project-setup.md
|-- 02-ui-identity.md
|-- blueprint.yaml
|-- 03-phases/
|   |-- phase-index.yaml
|   |-- phase-flow.md
|   `-- 01-...md
`-- HANDOVER.md</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="capability" class="docs-doc-section">
          <h2>Capability Buildprint</h2>
          <p>Use this when the agent is adding one bounded capability to an existing app, for example RBAC, Stripe subscriptions, search, or audit logging.</p>
          <pre><code>capability-buildprint/
|-- BUILDPRINT.md
|-- capability.yaml
|-- compatibility.md
|-- apply.md
|-- verify.md
|-- 00-host-assessment.md
|-- 01-integration-plan.md
`-- 02-implementation-phases/
    |-- 01-contract-and-config.md
    |-- 02-core-integration.md
    |-- 03-host-wiring.md
    `-- 05-verification-and-receipt.md</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="read-order" class="docs-doc-section">
          <h2>Read order</h2>
          <p><code>BUILDPRINT.md</code> is the authority. It should tell the agent what to read next, which assumptions to resolve, which phase gates apply, and what proof is required.</p>
          <ol>
            <li>Read <code>BUILDPRINT.md</code>.</li>
            <li>Answer alignment questions before implementation.</li>
            <li>Follow setup and constraints.</li>
            <li>Run phases in order.</li>
            <li>Run verification and write the handover.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <section id="install-agb" class="docs-doc-section">
          <h2>Install AGB</h2>
          <p>AGB is the loader CLI. It downloads the exact Buildprint files into <code>.buildprint/</code> so the agent does not scrape UI cards or copy files by hand.</p>
          <pre><code>npm install -g agent-buildprint
agb --help</code></pre>
          <p>If you do not want a global install, clone the source repo and run the local binary.</p>
          <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/DomEscobar/agent-buildprint
node agent-buildprint/bin/agb.js --help</code></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="getting-started" class="docs-doc-section">
          <h2>Use a Buildprint</h2>
          <p>Start from an existing packet, load exact files, then ask the coding agent to follow the Buildprint instead of guessing from a loose prompt.</p>
          <pre><code>mkdir rbac-build
cd rbac-build
agb start https://agent-buildprint.com/buildprints/rbac-permissions/package.json .

# then tell the agent to read:
.buildprint/next-agent.md</code></pre>
          <p>Without the global install, use the local binary instead.</p>
          <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/DomEscobar/agent-buildprint
node agent-buildprint/bin/agb.js start \
  https://agent-buildprint.com/buildprints/rbac-permissions/package.json .

# then read:
.buildprint/next-agent.md</code></pre>
          <p>Manual path: open a Buildprint page, read <code>BUILDPRINT.md</code>, follow the named files in order, run verification, then write the final handover.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="create-buildprint" class="docs-doc-section">
          <h2>Create a Buildprint with a Buildprint</h2>
          <p>To author a new reusable capability packet, start from the Capability Buildprint Author. That Buildprint creates Buildprints. It does not install the capability into an app.</p>
          <pre><code>mkdir author-stripe-buildprint
cd author-stripe-buildprint
agb start \
  https://agent-buildprint.com/buildprints/capability-buildprint-author/package.json .

# then tell the agent to read:
.buildprint/next-agent.md</code></pre>
          <p>Give the agent a specific capability target, not a vague feature. For example: <code>Create a Capability Buildprint for Stripe Checkout subscriptions with webhook-verified entitlement state in compatible Next.js apps.</code></p>
          <p>The expected output is a new capability folder with <code>BUILDPRINT.md</code>, <code>capability.yaml</code>, compatibility rules, apply/verify instructions, implementation phases, and README metadata.</p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
